1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:12,520
President Pittman, Mr. Vice President, Governor, Congressman Thomas, Senator Wiley, and Congressman

2
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:20,980
Miller, Mr. Webb, Bell, scientists, distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen.

3
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:26,960
I appreciate your president having made me an honorary visiting professor and I will

4
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:31,230
assure you that my first lecture will be very brief.

5
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:36,740
I am delighted to be here and I am particularly delighted to be here on this occasion.

6
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:44,460
We meet at a college, noted for knowledge, in a city, noted for progress, in a state,

7
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:49,460
noted for strength, and we stand in need of all praise.

8
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:56,640
We meet in an hour of change and challenge, in a decade of hope and fear, in an age

9
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:59,580
of both knowledge and ignorance.

10
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:05,500
The greater our knowledge increases, the greater our ignorance unfolds.

11
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:11,200
Despite the striking fact that most of the scientists that the world has ever known are

12
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:19,100
alive and working today, despite the fact that this nation's own scientific manpower

13
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:26,210
is doubling every 12 years in a rate of growth more than three times that of our

14
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:34,110
population as a whole, despite that the vast stretches of the unknown and the

15
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:42,440
unanswered and the unfinished still far outstrip our collective comprehension.

16
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:51,620
No man can fully grasp how far and how fast we have come, but condense, if you will,

17
00:01:51,660 --> 00:02:00,450
the 50,000 years of man's recorded history in a time span of but a half a century.

18
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:06,910
Stated in these terms, we know very little about the first 40 years, except at the end

19
00:02:06,910 --> 00:02:12,900
of them, advanced man had learned to use the skins of animals to cover them.

20
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:19,810
Then about 10 years ago, under this standard, man emerged from his cage to construct

21
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:21,910
other kinds of shelter.

22
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:28,620
Only five years ago, man learned to write and use a cart with wheels.

23
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:32,600
Christianity began less than two years ago.

24
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,640
The printing press came this year.

25
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:42,700
And then less than two months ago, during this whole 50-year span of human history,

26
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:47,060
the steam engine provided a new source of power.

27
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,940
Newton explored the meaning of gravity.

28
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:57,880
Last month, electric lights and telephones and automobiles and airplanes became available.

29
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:04,640
Only last week did we develop penicillin and television and nuclear power.

30
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:10,880
And now if America's new spacecraft succeeds in reaching Venus,

31
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:16,030
we will have literally reached the stars before midnight tonight.

32
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:18,420
This is a breathtaking pace.

33
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:25,620
And such a pace cannot help but create new ills, as it dispels old.

34
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:29,930
New ignorance, new problems, new dangers.

35
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:38,770
Surely the opening vistas of space promise high cost and hardships as well as high reward.

36
00:03:38,770 --> 00:03:45,220
So it is not surprising that some would have us stay where we are a little longer

37
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,650
to rest, to wait.

38
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:53,610
But this city of Houston, this state of Texas, this country of the United States

39
00:03:53,610 --> 00:03:58,690
was not built by those who waited and rested and wished to look behind them.

40
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:13,710
This country was conquered by those who moved forward, and so will space.

41
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:21,550
William Brantford, speaking in 1630 of the founding of the Plymouth Bay Colony,

42
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:27,670
said that all great and honorable actions are accompanied with great difficulties.

43
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:34,120
And both must be enterprise and overcome with answerable courage.

44
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:39,120
If this capital history of our progress teaches us anything,

45
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:45,120
it is that man in his quest for knowledge and progress is determined and cannot be deterred.

46
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:51,830
The exploration of space will go ahead, whether we join in it or not.

47
00:04:51,830 --> 00:04:55,830
And it is one of the great adventures of all time.

48
00:04:55,830 --> 00:05:01,080
And no nation which expects to be the leader of other nations

49
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:05,080
can expect to stay behind in this race for space.

50
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:14,400
Those who came before us made certain that this country roved the first waves of the industrial revolution,

51
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:19,400
the first waves of modern inventions, and the first wave of nuclear power.

52
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:27,400
And this generation does not intend to founder in the backwash of the coming age of space.

53
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:30,400
We mean to be a part of it. We mean to lead it.

54
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:49,630
For the eyes of the world now look into space to the moon and to the planets beyond.

55
00:05:49,630 --> 00:05:58,080
And we have vowed that we shall not see it governed by a hostile flag of conquest,

56
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:02,080
but by a banner of freedom and peace.

57
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:08,270
We have vowed that we shall not see space filled with weapons of mass destruction,

58
00:06:08,270 --> 00:06:12,370
but with instruments of knowledge and understanding.

59
00:06:12,370 --> 00:06:20,470
Yet the vows of this nation can only be fulfilled if we in this nation are first,

60
00:06:20,470 --> 00:06:30,280
and therefore we intend to be first.

61
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:37,600
In short, our leadership in science and industry, our hopes for peace and security,

62
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:44,600
our obligations to ourselves as well as others all require us to make this effort

63
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:49,790
to thaw these mysteries, to thaw them for the good of all men,

64
00:06:49,790 --> 00:06:55,080
and to become the world's leading space-faring nation.

65
00:06:55,080 --> 00:07:01,080
We set sail on this new sea because there is new knowledge to be gained

66
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:08,080
and new rights to be won, and they must be won and used for the progress of all people.

67
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:13,400
For space science, like nuclear science and all technology,

68
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:16,590
has no conscience of its own.

69
00:07:16,590 --> 00:07:21,750
Whether it will become a force for good or ill depends on man,

70
00:07:21,750 --> 00:07:28,360
and only if the United States occupies a position of preeminence

71
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:34,910
can we help decide whether this new ocean will be a sea of peace

72
00:07:34,910 --> 00:07:37,910
or a new terrifying theater of war.

73
00:07:37,910 --> 00:07:43,290
I do not say that we should or will go unprotected

74
00:07:43,290 --> 00:07:49,350
against the hostile misuse of space any more than we go unprotected

75
00:07:49,350 --> 00:07:52,350
against the hostile use of land or seas.

76
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:57,350
But I do say that space can be explored and mastered

77
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:00,390
without feeding the fires of war,

78
00:08:00,390 --> 00:08:04,390
without repeating the mistakes that man has made in extending his rites.

79
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:06,690
around this globe of ours.

80
00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:10,690
There is no strike, no prejudice,

81
00:08:10,690 --> 00:08:13,690
no national conflict in outer space as yet.

82
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:17,040
It's hazard to hostile to us all.

83
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:21,230
Its conquest deserves the best of all mankind.

84
00:08:21,230 --> 00:08:26,230
And its opportunity for peaceful cooperation may never come again.

85
00:08:26,230 --> 00:08:29,840
But why some say the moon?

86
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:31,840
Why choose this as our goal?

87
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:36,290
And they may well ask why climb the highest mountain?

88
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:40,420
Why 35 years ago fly the Atlantic?

89
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:42,420
Why does rice play Texas?

90
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:45,420
We choose to go to the moon.

91
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:47,640
We choose to go to the moon.

92
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:58,020
We choose to go to the moon in this decay and do the other things.

93
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:02,020
Not because they are easy, but because they are hard.

94
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:07,020
Because that goal will serve to organize and measure the best

95
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:09,020
of our energies and skills.

96
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:13,020
Because that challenge is one that we are willing to accept.

97
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:16,050
One we are unwilling to postpone.

98
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:18,050
And one we intend to win.

99
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:27,060
And the other too.

100
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:31,060
It is for these reasons that I regard the decision last year

101
00:09:31,060 --> 00:09:36,090
to shift our efforts in space from low to high gear

102
00:09:36,090 --> 00:09:39,090
as among the most important decisions that will be made

103
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:42,090
during my incumbency in the office of the presidency.

104
00:09:42,090 --> 00:09:47,250
In the last 24 hours we have seen facilities now being created

105
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:52,250
for the greatest and most complex exploration in man's history.

106
00:09:52,250 --> 00:09:56,410
We have felt the ground shake and the air shattered

107
00:09:56,410 --> 00:10:00,410
by the testing of a Saturn C1 booster rocket.

108
00:10:00,410 --> 00:10:05,570
Many times as powerful as the Atlas which launched John Glenn.

109
00:10:05,570 --> 00:10:09,600
Generating power equivalents to 10,000 automobiles

110
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:12,730
with their accelerator on the floor.

111
00:10:12,730 --> 00:10:17,730
We have seen the site where five F1 rocket engines,

112
00:10:17,730 --> 00:10:22,760
each one as powerful as all eight engines of the Saturn combined

113
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:28,760
will be clustered together to make the advanced Saturn missile

114
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:32,790
assembled in a new building to be built at Cape Canaveral

115
00:10:32,790 --> 00:10:38,790
as tall as a 48-story structure, as wide as a city block

116
00:10:38,790 --> 00:10:42,790
and as long as two lengths of this field.

117
00:10:42,790 --> 00:10:48,790
Within these last 19 months, at least 45 satellites have circled the Earth.

118
00:10:48,790 --> 00:10:52,830
Some 40 of them were made in the United States of America

119
00:10:52,830 --> 00:10:55,830
and they were far more sophisticated

120
00:10:55,830 --> 00:10:58,830
and supplied far more knowledge to the people of the world

121
00:10:58,830 --> 00:11:01,890
than those of the Soviet Union.

122
00:11:01,890 --> 00:11:11,880
The Marinus spacecraft, the Marinus spacecraft now in its way to Venus

123
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:16,880
is the most intricate instrument in the history of space science.

124
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:22,880
The accuracy of that shot is comparable to firing a missile from Cape Canaveral

125
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:27,230
and dropping it in this stadium between the 40-yard lines.

126
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:32,390
Transit satellites are helping our ships at sea to steer a safer course.

127
00:11:32,390 --> 00:11:37,390
Tireless satellites have given us unprecedented warnings of hurricanes and storms

128
00:11:37,390 --> 00:11:41,390
and will do the same for forest fires and icebergs.

129
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:46,390
We have had our failures but so have others even if they do not admit them

130
00:11:46,390 --> 00:11:48,390
and they may be less public.

131
00:11:48,390 --> 00:11:56,520
To be sure, you'll be sure we are behind

132
00:11:56,520 --> 00:12:00,520
and will be behind for some time in man's flight

133
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:05,520
but we do not intend to stay behind and in this decade we shall make up.

134
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:19,340
and move ahead. The growth of our science and education will be enriched by new knowledge

135
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:26,000
of our universe and environment, by new techniques of learning and mapping and observation, by

136
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,560
new tools and computers for industry, medicine, and the whole as well as the school. Technical

137
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:38,820
institutions such as Rice will reap the harvest of these gains. And finally, the

138
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:46,120
space effort itself, while still in its infancy, has already created a great number of new companies.

139
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:53,370
And tens of thousands of new jobs, space and related industries are generating new demands

140
00:12:53,370 --> 00:13:00,450
in investment and skilled personnel. And this city and this state and this region will

141
00:13:00,450 --> 00:13:06,250
share greatly in this role. What was once the furthest outpost on the old frontier

142
00:13:06,250 --> 00:13:18,710
of the West will be the furthest outpost on the new frontier of science and space. Houston,

143
00:13:18,710 --> 00:13:26,710
your city of Houston, with its manned spacecraft center, will become the heart of a large scientific

144
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:32,740
and engineering community. During the next five years, the National Aeronautic and Space

145
00:13:32,740 --> 00:13:38,840
Administration expects to double the number of scientists and engineers in this area

146
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:45,200
to increase its outlays for salaries and expenses to $60 million a year, to invest some

147
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:53,000
$200 million in plans and laboratory facilities, and to direct a contract for new space efforts

148
00:13:53,000 --> 00:14:00,160
over $1 billion from this center in this city. To be sure all this costs us all a good

149
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:08,800
deal of money. This year's space budget is three times what it was in January 1961

150
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:14,280
and it is greater than the space budget of the previous eight years combined. That budget

151
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:23,480
now stands at $5,400 million a year, a staggering sum, though somewhat less than we pay for cigarettes

152
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:36,640
and cigars every year. Space expenditures, space expenditures will soon rise some more

153
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:43,280
from 40 cents per person per week to more than 50 cents a week for every man, woman

154
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:50,790
and child in the United States. For we have given this program a high national priority,

155
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:56,670
even though I realize that this is in some measure an act of faith and vision. For we

156
00:14:56,670 --> 00:15:03,840
do not now know what benefits the weight of us. But if I were to say my fellow citizens

157
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:10,850
that we shall send to the moon 240,000 miles away from the control station in

158
00:15:10,850 --> 00:15:18,810
Houston, a giant rocket more than 300 feet tall, the length of this football field, made

159
00:15:18,810 --> 00:15:25,200
of new metal alloys, some of which have not yet been invented, capable of standing heat

160
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:31,050
and stresses, several times more than it's ever been experienced, fitted together with

161
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:37,290
a precision better than the finest watch, carrying all the equipment needed for propulsion,

162
00:15:37,290 --> 00:15:45,000
guidance, control, communication, food and survival, on an untried mission to an unknown

163
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,480
celestial body and then return it safely to Earth, re-entering the atmosphere at speeds

164
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:57,480
of over 25,000 miles per hour, causing heat about half that on the temperature of the

165
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:03,520
sun, almost as hot as it is here today, and do all this, and do all this, and do

166
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:05,520
it right, and do it first.

167
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:18,960
Before this decade is out, then we must evolve.

168
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:24,500
I'm the one ensuring all the work, so we just want to stay cool for a minute.

169
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:26,500
However, I think we're going to do it.

170
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:29,500
And I think that we must pay what needs to be paid.

171
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:33,500
I don't think we ought to waste any money, but I think we ought to do the job.

172
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:36,500
And this will be done in the decade of your 60s.

173
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:40,500
It may be done while some of you are still here at school at this college and university.

174
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:45,500
It will be done during the terms of office with some of the people who sit here on this platform.

175
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:49,500
But it will be done, and it will be done before the end of this decade.

176
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:54,500
And I am delighted that this university is playing a part in putting a man on the moon

177
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:58,500
as part of a great national effort of the United States of America.

178
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:14,370
Many years ago, the great British explorer, George Mallory, who was to die on Mount Everest,

179
00:17:14,370 --> 00:17:16,370
was asked why did he want to climb it.

180
00:17:16,370 --> 00:17:18,500
He said because it is there.

181
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:20,600
Well, space is there.

182
00:17:20,600 --> 00:17:22,630
And we're going to climb it.

183
00:17:22,630 --> 00:17:24,630
And the moon and the planets are there.

184
00:17:24,630 --> 00:17:27,630
And new hopes for knowledge and peace are there.

185
00:17:27,630 --> 00:17:34,790
And therefore, as we set sail, we ask God's blessings on the most hazardous and dangerous

186
00:17:34,790 --> 00:17:37,790
and greatest adventure on which man has ever embarked.

187
00:17:37,790 --> 00:17:38,790
Thank you.
